# Runtime state: snapshots of threads, mail drafts, scratch letters.
# Rebuilt from observation on every tick; nothing here is a record of decision.
state/

# Live product content of this installation: snapshots, the portfolio plan,
# decisions and attachments. Git owns the portable core that reads and writes
# this — the schema, the migration and the tests — and never the content
# itself, so product work cannot change the evidence base of a code review.
# Its only durable copy is the nightly backup, which fails closed when this
# root is missing; `product_memory.py --check` reads it back.
content/

# The retired product monoliths. They stay on disk as the dated archive and the
# rollback path of the 2026-08-12 migration, byte-identical to the copies under
# `content/archive/`, and no consumer reads them any more. `product_memory.py
# --check` refuses when one of them diverges from the migration manifest, so a
# habitual edit here is observed instead of silently lost.
products/

# This installation's own file: where its task system stands, which address its
# letters go to, which directions it watches and which repositories each owns.
# `threads.example.json` is the shape; the answers are one installation's.
threads.json

# The wake-up offsets of this installation's own directions. The template unit,
# its timer and one example drop-in stay in git; a direction that exists only
# here does not.
systemd/product-thread@*.timer.d/
!systemd/product-thread@example.timer.d/

# The lessons this installation has learned, with the observation, the cost and
# the owner of each. `lesson.py` owns the mechanism and is in git; what one
# product owner has observed about one user is not.
lessons/inbox.json
lessons/applied.md

# Work that happens to be done in this checkout rather than in the task system.
tasks/
logs/

# Local machine settings and tool caches.
.claude/settings.local.json
.mcp.json
.pytest_cache/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
